      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785953788133,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1) How do we match player fairly at scale?",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1) How do we match player fairly at scale?",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "vKCrf2FGgvx1iCokz9ja5",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1108.390625,
      "y": 690.37890625,
      "width": 932.0992431640625,
      "height": 450,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aE",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 209489258,
      "version": 411,
      "versionNonce": 1989889514,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785954168031,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- At peak, 500k concurrent game ~ 1M playes,\n- Assume each player start fresh match 120s => 1M/120 = 8k new match request/second\n- Dealing with contention pattern:\n==> Two matchmaking workers can spot the same waiting player at the same instant \nand both try to pair them, which would double-book that player into two games. \nThat's a contention problem, and our dealing-with-contention pattern covers the atomic\n claim techniques that make sure exactly one worker wins.\n- Solution: ***Shared Redis Sorted Set ****\n-- Sorted set per time control in Redis, one copy of pending pool, any matchmaker can\nsearch and claim it\n-- Claiming the opponent. Finding a candidate isn't enough, because thousands of workers scan \nthe same hot pool and several can land on the same waiting player at once. \nThe claim has to be atomic, and ZREM gives us that for free since it returns \nthe number of members it actually removed. A worker that gets 1 pulled that player out, \nwhile a worker that gets 0 knows somebody beat it there.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- At peak, 500k concurrent game ~ 1M playes,\n- Assume each player start fresh match 120s => 1M/120 = 8k new match request/second\n- Dealing with contention pattern:\n==> Two matchmaking workers can spot the same waiting player at the same instant \nand both try to pair them, which would double-book that player into two games. \nThat's a contention problem, and our dealing-with-contention pattern covers the atomic\n claim techniques that make sure exactly one worker wins.\n- Solution: ***Shared Redis Sorted Set ****\n-- Sorted set per time control in Redis, one copy of pending pool, any matchmaker can\nsearch and claim it\n-- Claiming the opponent. Finding a candidate isn't enough, because thousands of workers scan \nthe same hot pool and several can land on the same waiting player at once. \nThe claim has to be atomic, and ZREM gives us that for free since it returns \nthe number of members it actually removed. A worker that gets 1 pulled that player out, \nwhile a worker that gets 0 knows somebody beat it there.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "u-tu-X8LXRuCPIGZhRLOg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1116.7953186035156,
      "y": 1094.109375,
      "width": 258.89984130859375,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
