    },
    {
      "id": "3_KayJgzUbFp-zw2GZ60t",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1125.5086111007051,
      "y": 2344.2102258916593,
      "width": 1023.2992553710938,
      "height": 125,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aR",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2057747062,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 1421684918,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785955232531,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-  keep the ranking in a Redis sorted set, with the member as the playerId and the score as the rating. \n- The write rate is just the game-end rate, roughly 4k games finishing per second times two players, \nso about 8k ZADDs per second, which Redis handles easily. \nA global ranking wants one sorted structure anyway, so there's nothing to shard.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-  keep the ranking in a Redis sorted set, with the member as the playerId and the score as the rating. \n- The write rate is just the game-end rate, roughly 4k games finishing per second times two players, \nso about 8k ZADDs per second, which Redis handles easily. \nA global ranking wants one sorted structure anyway, so there's nothing to shard.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RgsZAVil9y71xWrZ9M1aF",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1119.6864108687632,
      "y": 2535.6928770975273,
      "width": 1025.8573474790728,
      "height": 774.5184057642714,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aS",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1686333686,
      "version": 977,
      "versionNonce": 928539830,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1785955356679,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Engine assistance is the existential threat to online chess, and a player feeding the live position to an\nengine is nearly undetectable from any single game. Catching it is an offline ML and behavioral-analysis\nproblem, comparing move choices against engine top-picks, watching move-time patterns, and scoring\naccuracy against rating history, then flagging accounts for review. It's a different system from the real-\ntime path we built, which is why it's below the line. But naming it (along with tournament integrity)\nshows you understand what really makes or breaks one of these platforms.\n\n\n\nA top game, a super-GM blitz match, can draw tens of thousands of watchers, which is a read fan-out\nproblem with nothing in common with the 1:1 gameplay path. You wouldn't hang spectators off the\nauthoritative game server. You'd fan validated moves out through a pub/sub layer, or a CDN-style tree,\nto read-only subscribers, where a few hundred milliseconds of lag is fine because spectators never write.\n\n\nEvery finished game is kept forever (Lichess sits on more than 12 billion), which is a data-at-rest\nproblem separate from the live path. It powers post-game analysis and the opening explorer, the \"what\ndo players usually play in this position\" feature. This is easier than it first looks, because a game is\njust its move sequence stored as a string. Finding every game that followed a given line is a prefix range\nscan over those sequences rather than anything fancy. Richer queries across the whole archive,\naggregating by position including transpositions, are where you'd reach for a columnar or search store\n(Lichess indexes games in Elasticsearch). None of it belongs on the OLTP database the live game runs\non.\n\n\n\nIn bullet and ultrabullet, players queue a move to fire the instant the opponent moves, and strong\nplayers stack several in a row. What makes it interesting is the server logic. The server validates and\napplies a move the player committed before they'd seen the opponent's actual reply, and discards it\ncleanly when the reply makes it illegal. All of that happens the moment the opponent's move lands, so the\nqueued move costs effectively no clock. Real platforms have iterated on these edge cases for years.",
      "fontSize": 19.987571761658636,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Engine assistance is the existential threat to online chess, and a player feeding the live position to an engine is nearly undetectable from any single game. Catching it is an offline ML and behavioral-analysis problem, comparing move choices against engine top-picks, watching move-time patterns, and scoring accuracy against rating history, then flagging accounts for review. It's a different system from the real-time path we built, which is why it's below the line. But naming it (along with tournament integrity) shows you understand what really makes or breaks one of these platforms.\n\n\n\nA top game, a super-GM blitz match, can draw tens of thousands of watchers, which is a read fan-out problem with nothing in common with the 1:1 gameplay path. You wouldn't hang spectators off the authoritative game server. You'd fan validated moves out through a pub/sub layer, or a CDN-style tree, to read-only subscribers, where a few hundred milliseconds of lag is fine because spectators never write.\n\n\nEvery finished game is kept forever (Lichess sits on more than 12 billion), which is a data-at-rest problem separate from the live path. It powers post-game analysis and the opening explorer, the \"what do players usually play in this position\" feature. This is easier than it first looks, because a game is just its move sequence stored as a string. Finding every game that followed a given line is a prefix range scan over those sequences rather than anything fancy. Richer queries across the whole archive, aggregating by position including transpositions, are where you'd reach for a columnar or search store (Lichess indexes games in Elasticsearch). None of it belongs on the OLTP database the live game runs on.\n\n\n\nIn bullet and ultrabullet, players queue a move to fire the instant the opponent moves, and strong players stack several in a row. What makes it interesting is the server logic. The server validates and applies a move the player committed before they'd seen the opponent's actual reply, and discards it cleanly when the reply makes it illegal. All of that happens the moment the opponent's move lands, so the queued move costs effectively no clock. Real platforms have iterated on these edge cases for years.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
